nodecode-call-me is a rewrite of radres's radres/hermes-plugin-call-me:

  https://github.com/radres/hermes-plugin-call-me
  commit 970952e72d633ebdbed20d8c60c327cc45475140
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Serdar Oztetik (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the two verbs the original gave an agent — a text to the
operator's phone through the /call-me service, and a call that speaks a
sentence out loud and returns the spoken answer as a transcript — as the
callme_text and callme_call tools, the /callme command that says what the
number reaches and sends a text or places a call now, and a manual topic
(help :call-me) in place of the original's gateway platform prompt. Kept
with them: the agent session the service mints and the one session file it
is kept in, so the agent's own number is stable; the allowlist that fences
both verbs, so a number or name outside CALLME_ALLOWED_USERS is refused
before any request leaves; the name-or-number recipient, empty when one
number is allowed; the 2000-character ceiling on what is texted and spoken;
the 30-to-300-second call wait and the "missed" answer that tells the model
to send a text instead; and the service's own words for a refusal. The
Python became Common Lisp — nlk:http for the two request shapes instead of
aiohttp and httpx, one section declaration for the settings instead of five
environment variables and the .env wizard, and nlk:define-contract for each
tool's arguments, so the schema the model is shown and the body that runs
are one shape. Nothing else is read: the cell touches no file but its own
session file, and no environment variable at all.

Left out: the inbound half — the original registers itself as a chat
platform, long-polls the service's event stream, greets each allowed number
once, gates inbound messages on the service's verified-device mark, dedupes
them and answers with a chunked reply, and lets cron deliver its output to a
home number. That is a room, and a room needs the bot plumbing this folder
does not carry; here the agent texts and rings, and a person's reply arrives
on their phone in the /call-me thread rather than in the session. Also left
out: the voicemail transcription event, the single flight of a session held
in memory across a long-running gateway (a fresh process re-reads the same
file, which is what matters here), and CALLME_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED, a switch for
old app builds whose inbound messages carry no verified mark — with no
inbound path, there is nothing for it to let in.
